---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LeadForm from "../components/LeadForm.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";

const title = "Water damage restoration in Hamilton Mill, GA";
const description =
  "Burst pipe, leak, or storm water in Hamilton Mill, off I-85 at Exit 120. Call or send what happened.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <p class="muted">Gwinnett County</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Water where it doesn't belong? We're here to help. Call or click "Request
      help in Hamilton Mill" now and fill out the form. Share as much as you can
      about the problem and we will get back to you as soon as we can.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#request">Request help in Hamilton Mill</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/what-to-do">First-hour checklist</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Water damage in Hamilton Mill</h2>
    <p>
      Hamilton Mill sits off I-85 at Exit 120. Your mail may say Dacula, because
      the ZIP code is 30019. The area is also now the City of Mulberry, which
      voters approved in 2024. But people who live here say Hamilton Mill, and
      it is its own place.
    </p>
    <p>
      Homes here started going up in the mid-1990s and kept going for years.
      That makes many of them 20 to 30 years old. Water heaters, roofs, and air
      conditioners from the first years are now on their second life, or past
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the usual call is not a flood. It is a water heater that let go in the
      garage, a stain on the ceiling under an upstairs bathroom, or a wet spot
      on the carpet that nobody can explain.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a two-story house, a leak upstairs does not stay upstairs. Water from a
      bathroom or laundry room can run through the floor and down the walls to
      the rooms below. By the time a stain shows on the ceiling, the damage has
      often spread past where the water started.
    </p>
    <p>
      Water does not always stay where it lands. On a slab, it spreads across
      the floor and soaks up into the walls. In a house with a finished
      basement, a leak upstairs can come down through the ceiling and pool
      behind the drywall where you can't see it.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the first questions are about your house. When you call or
      send the form, tell us where the water came from, which rooms are wet, and
      how long it has been there. Tell us if the house has a basement or is on a
      slab. If you are not sure, say so. That is enough to start.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storms do some damage too. A hard rain can push water in at a window, a
      door, or a bad spot in the roof. In Georgia, most of the house damage from
      Hurricane Helene came from falling trees, not floodwater. A damaged roof
      can leak for days before the stain shows up on the ceiling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where people say they live</h2>
    <p>
      Hamilton Mill is one master-planned community with many sections. The golf
      course runs through the middle of it, and the sections have their own
      names.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Highpointe</strong> was built around 1996 to 2000.
      <strong>Glenaire</strong> came later, around 2002.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rest include The Enclave, Grove Park, Laurel Ridge, Lake Forest,
      Fairway Bend, Millwater Crossing, Town Manor, HuntField, Ashbrooke,
      Brookside, Cambridge, Coventry, Parkside, Ridge Brooke, Treybyrne,
      WindRidge, and WoodLeaf.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most of the homes are single-family houses. Some sit on a slab. Some have
      a basement, either full, partial, or a walkout, and you see more of those
      on the hillside lots in the later sections. Check your own house before
      you assume.
    </p>
    <p>
      You will find Hamilton Mill along Hamilton Mill Parkway, Braselton
      Highway, Hog Mountain Road, and Jim Moore Road.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Common causes in Hamilton Mill</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Water heater.</strong> The tank leaks or lets go in a garage or closet.
        This is common in houses from the late 1990s and early 2000s.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Air conditioner drain.</strong> The drain line clogs and the pan overflows.
        The water comes through the ceiling below it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refrigerator or washer line.</strong> The hose cracks. Water slides
        under the floor, and you smell it before you see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Burst pipe.</strong> After a hard freeze, a pipe can split in a wall
        or an attic. Gwinnett had a lot of these in December 2022.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Leak that reaches the basement.</strong> Water from upstairs runs down
        into a finished basement. Basement cleanup takes longer than a one-room leak.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storm water.</strong> Rain gets in through a window, a door, or a roof
        damaged by wind or a falling tree.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets.</strong> Do not clean these
        up yourself. Call.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>If water is in the house now</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the water off, if you can do it safely. Stay away from wet outlets.</li>
      <li>Take photos of the water, the room, and where it started.</li>
      <li>
        Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets: <CallLink prefix="call" /> or
        <a href="#request">send the form</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Already stopped a small drip? Use the
        <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Call your insurance company when you can. If you plan to file a claim, do
      not start cutting out drywall first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not wait until tomorrow to dry things out. Wet drywall and flooring get
      worse overnight, and mold can start in 24 to 48 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Questions from Hamilton Mill homeowners</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <div>
        <dt>
          I found a leak and I do not know where my main water shut-off is. What
          do I do?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Look where the water line comes into the house. It is often in the
          garage, a basement, or a closet near the water heater. A round handle
          turns clockwise to close. A lever turns a quarter turn, until it sits
          across the pipe. If the leak is at one fixture, look for the small
          valve under the sink or behind the toilet first. If you cannot find
          any of them, say so when you call.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          There is a brown stain on my upstairs ceiling and no pipe is above it.
          What is it?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          It may be the air conditioner. When the drain line clogs, the water
          backs up and spills out of the pan, and it can run through the ceiling
          below. Turn the air conditioner off so it stops making more water.
          Take a photo, put a bucket under the drip, and call if the ceiling is
          wet, sagging, or the stain is spreading.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>My leak was upstairs. Do I need to check downstairs too?</dt>
        <dd>
          Yes. Water goes down through the floor and into the walls. Look at the
          ceiling in the room below and at the tops of the walls. Press on the
          drywall. If it is soft, stained, or bubbling, there is water behind
          it, even if the room feels dry. Tell us which room the leak started
          in.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          My basement floor is wet after a hard rain. Is it a leak or the
          weather?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Look at where the water is coming from. If it comes through the wall
          or up through the floor after rain, it is outside water. If it is near
          a pipe, the water heater, or the ceiling, it is a leak. Either way it
          can soak carpet and drywall, so take photos and call. Outside water
          and burst pipes are often covered differently in an insurance policy,
          so ask your insurer.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          My water heater is the one that came with the house. Should I replace
          it before it leaks?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          It is a good question to ask before it becomes a 2 a.m. call. Tanks
          wear out. Look at the bottom for rust, drips, or a puddle. If it is
          the original and you are not sure how old it is, it is worth having a
          plumber look at it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          We came home from a trip and a pipe froze and split while we were gone.
          What do we do?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Shut off the water at the main. Take photos of everything before you
          move it. Then call. The longer water sits in carpet and drywall, the
          bigger the job gets, and a house that has been wet for days needs more
          than fans.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Questions about drying time, insurance, or whether you have to hire the
      company that calls are answered on <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> and
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p>
      <strong>What a job looks like.</strong> A crew checks the house, pulls out
      standing water, dries the structure, and replaces what can't be saved. You
      should be shown moisture readings, not just told it looks dry. How long it
      takes depends on the job. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clean water, dirty water, sewage.</strong> A broken supply line is
      not the same as a washer overflow or a sewer backup. Say which one it is on
      the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Insurance.</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a failed appliance is often
      covered. A slow leak, outside water, or sewage may not be unless you bought extra
      coverage. Take photos first. Don't tear out drywall to "help." Ask your insurer.
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;" id="request">
    <h2>Request help</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name, phone, city, ZIP, and what happened. Someone will call you back.
    </p>
    <LeadForm defaultCity="hamilton-mill" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h2>Nearby</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/dacula">Dacula</a> ·
      <a href="/lawrenceville">Lawrenceville</a> ·
      <a href="/buford">Buford</a> ·
      <a href="/suwanee">Suwanee</a> ·
      <a href="/gwinnett-county">Gwinnett County</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
